1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,804
Promises and fetch, without await. On the last exam this was a 30

2
00:00:03,904 --> 00:00:07,528
point problem, and the twist is that you have to write it with

3
00:00:07,628 --> 00:00:11,252
dot then instead of await. Let's make sure that feels natural.

4
00:00:13,352 --> 00:00:16,480
A promise is an object that stands for a result that

5
00:00:16,580 --> 00:00:20,082
isn't ready yet. You create one with new Promise, and pass

6
00:00:20,182 --> 00:00:23,932
it a function that receives two callbacks, resolve and reject.

7
00:00:24,010 --> 00:00:27,023
Every promise starts out pending. Calling resolve

8
00:00:27,123 --> 00:00:29,310
moves it to fulfilled, with a value.

9
00:00:29,410 --> 00:00:32,622
Calling reject moves it to rejected, with a reason.

10
00:00:32,710 --> 00:00:36,516
And it settles only once. After the first resolve or reject,

11
00:00:36,616 --> 00:00:40,357
any later calls are simply ignored. To react to the result,

12
00:00:40,457 --> 00:00:43,938
you use dot then for success and dot catch for failure.

13
00:00:46,010 --> 00:00:49,562
Here's a question the exam likes. This promise's function

14
00:00:49,662 --> 00:00:52,702
runs and logs work done, but never calls resolve.

15
00:00:52,777 --> 00:00:55,965
A little later we print the promise. What state is it in?

16
00:00:56,343 --> 00:00:59,483
It's still pending. And it'll stay pending forever.

17
00:01:00,577 --> 00:01:04,221
Exam trap. Doing the work doesn't settle a promise. The only

18
00:01:04,321 --> 00:01:07,965
things that settle it are calling resolve or calling reject.

19
00:01:11,043 --> 00:01:15,163
Here's the actual exam problem. getImageURL takes an index and a

20
00:01:15,263 --> 00:01:18,459
JSON file name, and it has to return a promise. So

21
00:01:18,559 --> 00:01:21,887
the very first thing we write is return new Promise.

22
00:01:21,985 --> 00:01:24,525
Inside, we fetch the file. fetch itself

23
00:01:24,625 --> 00:01:27,165
returns a promise of a response object.

24
00:01:27,252 --> 00:01:31,048
Calling response dot json returns another promise, this time

25
00:01:31,148 --> 00:01:34,165
of the parsed object. Returning it from the then

26
00:01:34,265 --> 00:01:37,088
means the next then receives the parsed JSON.

27
00:01:37,185 --> 00:01:40,509
Now the decision. If the index is past the end of the

28
00:01:40,609 --> 00:01:45,356
urls array, call reject with Invalid Index. Otherwise, resolve with the URL

29
00:01:45,456 --> 00:01:49,297
at that index. Those resolve and reject are the ones from our

30
00:01:49,397 --> 00:01:53,045
outer new Promise, so they settle the promise we returned.

31
00:01:55,418 --> 00:02:00,299
Using it looks like this. Index 1 is valid, so then receives the URL.

32
00:02:00,399 --> 00:02:04,918
In a real page, you'd set an image's src to that URL right here.

33
00:02:04,993 --> 00:02:08,193
Index 5 is past the end, so the promise rejects,

34
00:02:08,293 --> 00:02:11,493
the then is skipped, and catch gets the message.

35
00:02:11,893 --> 00:02:15,037
So we see b dot png, and Invalid Index. One honest

36
00:02:15,137 --> 00:02:18,930
note: these are two separate fetches, so with a real network

37
00:02:19,030 --> 00:02:21,849
the two lines could come out in either order.

38
00:02:22,927 --> 00:02:25,887
Exam trap. Don't forget the word return in front

39
00:02:25,987 --> 00:02:29,648
of new Promise. Without it, the function returns undefined,

40
00:02:29,748 --> 00:02:33,027
and calling dot then on undefined throws a TypeError.

41
00:02:36,093 --> 00:02:40,325
Two facts that show up in output questions. First, dot then itself

42
00:02:40,425 --> 00:02:45,182
returns a new promise. Whatever the callback returns becomes the value for

43
00:02:45,282 --> 00:02:48,857
the next then. Here 2 times 10 passes 20 down the chain.

44
00:02:48,943 --> 00:02:53,457
Second, an async function always returns a promise, even if you return

45
00:02:53,557 --> 00:02:57,147
a plain number. So logging f of nothing doesn't print 5.

46
00:02:57,243 --> 00:02:59,951
To actually get the 5, you use dot then.

47
00:03:00,343 --> 00:03:03,764
The first line is the promise object itself. Then 5,

48
00:03:03,864 --> 00:03:07,082
and then 20. The 20 comes last because that chain

49
00:03:07,182 --> 00:03:10,467
has two steps, and each step waits one extra turn.

50
00:03:13,543 --> 00:03:18,053
For comparison, here's the same function with async and await. await pauses

51
00:03:18,153 --> 00:03:21,987
this function until the promise settles and hands you the value.

52
00:03:22,060 --> 00:03:24,893
Throwing inside an async function rejects its

53
00:03:24,993 --> 00:03:27,696
promise, and returning a value fulfills it.

54
00:03:27,793 --> 00:03:30,885
This version is shorter, but read the question. If it

55
00:03:30,985 --> 00:03:33,957
says no await, you must write the dot then version.

56
00:03:34,327 --> 00:03:36,558
Index 9 is too big, so the throw

57
00:03:36,658 --> 00:03:40,347
rejects the promise, and catch prints Invalid Index.

58
00:03:43,427 --> 00:03:46,986
Promise dot all takes an array of promises and waits for all

59
00:03:47,086 --> 00:03:50,340
of them. The results come back as an array, in the same

60
00:03:50,440 --> 00:03:53,695
order as the input, no matter which one finished first.

61
00:03:53,777 --> 00:03:55,873
But if any one of them rejects, the

62
00:03:55,973 --> 00:03:58,573
whole thing rejects, and you land in catch.

63
00:03:58,943 --> 00:04:02,091
The first gives a dot png and c dot png,

64
00:04:02,191 --> 00:04:06,475
in order. The second fails because index 7 is invalid.

65
00:04:09,543 --> 00:04:14,645
Last pattern: turning a callback-style function, like setTimeout, into one

66
00:04:14,745 --> 00:04:18,371
that returns a promise. Step one, return new Promise.

67
00:04:18,460 --> 00:04:22,623
Step two, start the async task. Step three, inside that task's

68
00:04:22,723 --> 00:04:27,024
callback, call resolve with the result, or reject with an error.

69
00:04:27,093 --> 00:04:29,489
Now callers can use then and catch.

70
00:04:29,560 --> 00:04:31,727
And the smallest version of this idea is

71
00:04:31,827 --> 00:04:34,788
a wait function that resolves after some milliseconds.

72
00:04:35,160 --> 00:04:39,548
After one second, 16 for the number, and Not a number for the string.

73
00:04:42,627 --> 00:04:47,521
Practice question 1. Without await, write getTemperature, which takes a city and

74
00:04:47,621 --> 00:04:51,954
a JSON file and returns a promise. Resolve with the city's temperature,

75
00:04:52,054 --> 00:04:55,576
or reject with Unknown City if the city isn't in the file.

76
00:04:55,676 --> 00:04:58,823
Then show how to use it. Pause the video and try it.

77
00:05:07,093 --> 00:05:11,396
Same shape as getImageURL. Return new Promise, fetch the file,

78
00:05:11,496 --> 00:05:15,372
convert with response dot json, and then decide. If json

79
00:05:15,472 --> 00:05:20,059
of city is undefined, reject with Unknown City. Otherwise, resolve

80
00:05:20,159 --> 00:05:24,177
with the temperature. Then the caller uses then and catch.

81
00:05:28,260 --> 00:05:30,908
Practice question 2. What does this print, in

82
00:05:31,008 --> 00:05:32,984
order? Pause the video and try it.

83
00:05:41,260 --> 00:05:45,185
A, then D, then B 1. The function passed to new Promise

84
00:05:45,285 --> 00:05:50,307
runs immediately, so A prints first. The promise settles once, with 1,

85
00:05:50,407 --> 00:05:55,064
so reject 2 and resolve 3 are ignored. Then callbacks always wait

86
00:05:55,164 --> 00:05:59,016
until the current code finishes, so D prints before B.

87
00:06:03,093 --> 00:06:07,601
Recap. Return new Promise, with resolve and reject. Fetch, convert

88
00:06:07,701 --> 00:06:11,510
with response dot json, then decide whether to reject or

89
00:06:11,610 --> 00:06:15,768
resolve. Callers use then and catch. An async function always

90
00:06:15,868 --> 00:06:19,538
returns a promise. And Promise dot all keeps the input

91
00:06:19,638 --> 00:06:22,889
order, but fails as soon as one promise rejects.

